{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Qq2PyHbPpuazqYXbxoLChS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted data for each \"planned\" (RUNNING) experiment in the experiment plan, based on the \"best\" tier definition they provided.\n\nLooking at the registry, I can see two experiments with status \"RUNNING\":\n1. `w5_llama_docred_lora_constrained_eval` - Llama DocRED FT + constrained evaluation\n2. `w5_qwen_docred_lora_constrained_eval` - Qwen DocRED FT + constrained evaluation\n\nThe \"best\" tier definition says:\n- DR → 0% for both models (within [0.0, 0.1])\n\n\n- R-F1 preserved within ±0.2pp of unconstrained FT baseline (Llama ~43.7%, Qwen ~40.9%)\n- CF stays below 0.5%\n- Parse failure rate does not increase vs unconstrained FT (Qwen ≤7.1%, Llama ≤2.3%)\n\nNow I'm checking the unconstrained baselines to understand what the constrained versions should improve upon: Llama's unconstrained DocRED FT shows DR at 0.49%, RV at 0.15%, ET at 0%, R-F1 at 43.72%, E-F1 at 83.93%, and parse failure at 2.3%, while Qwen's baseline has DR at 0.76% with the rest of the metrics to follow.\n\nFor the \"best\" tier, I need DR to reach 0% (ideally 0.0-0.1 range), keep R-F1 within ±0.2pp, maintain CF below 0.5%, and ensure parse failure doesn't increase. Looking at how SciERC constrained performed gives me a reference point: Qwen achieved 0% DR with minimal R-F1 impact (−0.09pp) but CF jumped to 1.18%, while Llama nearly eliminated DR (0.09%) with negligible R-F1 change (+0.02pp) and CF stayed at 0.46%.\n\nNow I'm formulating the predictions for both running experiments based on this pattern—the expectation is that FT with constraints will replicate the SciERC results, driving DR to essentially zero while preserving F1 performance and confirming composability works.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"ca