{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Qq2PyHbPpuazqYXbxoLChS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: \"DocRED FT+constrained results mirror SciERC: DR→0%, F1 preserved, composability universally confirmed. Paper gains composability dimension — OntoCoDec is orthogonal to model improvement, works out of the box with FT at zero cost. C8 (low absolute F1) becomes non-issue since FT closes performance gap. Core claim: guaranteed type-validity as modular property persisting across training regimes.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Qwen DocRED LoRA FT + OntoCoDec Constrained Eval\"\n    plan_id: \"w5_qwen_docred_lora_constrained_eval\"\n    description: \"Apply full_s1 (grammar+semantic) constrained decoding to Qwen3-8B LoRA fine-tuned on DocRED. Validates composability: does OntoCoDec eliminate residual DR from FT model without degrading the 40.86% R-F1 gained through fine-tuning?\"\n    expected_results:\n      ET_pct: 0.0\n      RV_pct: 0.0\n      DR_pct: 0.0\n      CF_pct: 0.38\n      E_F1: 82.18\n      E_P: 85.48\n      E_R: 79.13\n      R_F1: 40.92\n      R_P: 36.47\n      R_R: 46.60\n      parse_fail_rate: 7.01\n      total_samples: 998\n      success_samples: 928\n      fail_samples: 70\n      total_entities_pred: 18095\n      total_relations_pred: 15862\n    result_rationale: >\n      FT already reduced DR from 18.46% (zero-shot) to 0.76% — the model learned ontology constraints\n      during training, leaving only ~120 residual DR violations across 15870 relations. These residual\n      violations are the \"long tail\" — relation types where the model occasionally assigns a borderline\n      entity type. OntoCoDec's semantic layer trivially catches these since (a) the FT model already\n      produces valid relation names (RV=0.27%), so grammar layer has minimal work, and (b) with near-perfect\n      vocabulary compliance, the semantic layer can reliably look up domain/range rules and mask the few\n      remaining violations. DR→0% follows the exact pattern seen on SciERC FT (Qwen DR 1.95%→0%).\n      \n      R-F1 preservation (Δ=+0.06pp) occurs because: (1) grammar eliminates the remaining 0.27% RV,\n      converting OOV relation predictions into valid ones — under all_set scoring this marginally helps\n      precision; (2) semantic masking on a well-calibrated FT model redirects probability mass from\n      the ~0.76% DR violations to nearby valid type assignments, which are often correct given FT's\n      strong calibration. Net effect is F1-neutral to marginally positive, within the ±0.2pp band.\n      \n      CF remains very low (0.38%) because FT's strong calibration means entity types are consistent\n      across contexts — the semantic layer rarely needs to override the model's type choice, so the\n      EVR mechanism that caused 9.9% CF in zero-shot (where the model was uncertain about types) is\n      largely absent. The few CF cases arise from genuinely ambiguous entities in DocRED's large ontology.\n      \n      Parse failure rate slightly improves (7.11%→7.01%) because grammar constraints ensure valid JSON\n      structure, rescuing a few documents that would have failed in unconstrained mode.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Qwen DocRED FT Composability Matrix\"\n        rows:\n          - condition: \"FT unconstrained\"\n            DR: 0.76\n            RV: 0.27\n            ET: 0.0\n            CF: 0.12\n            R_F1: 40.86\n            E_F1: 82.15\n          - condition: \"FT + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\"\n            DR: 0.0\n            RV: 0.0\n            ET: 0.0\n            CF: 0.38\n            R_F1: 40.92\n            E_F1: 82.18\n          - condition: \"Zero-shot + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\"\n            DR: 10.21\n            RV: 0.03\n            ET: 0.0\n            CF: 2.57\n            R_F1: 8.23\n            E_F1: 59.37\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart: DR reduction cascade — zero-shot (18.46%) → zero-shot+OntoCoDec (10.21%) → FT unconstrained (0.76%) → FT+OntoCoDec (0.0%). Shows FT and OntoCoDec as orthogonal DR eliminators.\"\n      - \"Scatter plot: per-relation DR before/after for FT+constrained. All 96 relations cluster at (x, 0) regardless of unconstrained residual rate, demonstrating universal elimination.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The Qwen DocRED FT+constrained result confirms the composability thesis at its strongest: OntoCoDec\n      eliminates the last 0.76% DR residual that FT could not learn away, while preserving the 5× R-F1\n      improvement (8.23→40.86→40.92) that FT provides. The two methods are genuinely orthogonal — FT\n      improves extraction quality, OntoCoDec guarantees type validity. Neither degrades the other.\n      \n      The near-zero CF (0.38% vs 9.9% in zero-shot) is the key new finding: it proves that zero-shot CF\n      was not an inherent limitation of OntoCoDec's architecture, but a consequence of applying semantic\n      constraints to an uncertain model. When the model is confident and well-calibrated (post-FT), the\n      semantic layer's overrides are rare and targeted, producing minimal consistency side-effects.\n      \n      Parse failure rate stability (7.11%→7.01%) confirms that constrained decoding does not create\n      new failure modes on FT models — a concern from zero-shot Llama where parse failures increased\n      from 6.8% to 9.1%.\n\n  - name: \"Llama DocRED LoRA FT + OntoCoDec Constrained Eval\"\n    plan_id: \"w5_llama_docred_lora_constrained_eval\"\n    description: \"Apply full_s1 (grammar+semantic) constrained decoding to Llama-3.1-8B LoRA fine-tuned on DocRED. Cross-model composability validation: does the composability pattern generalize beyond Qwen?\"\n    expected_results:\n      ET_pct: 0.0\n      RV_pct: 0.0\n      DR_pct: 0.03\n      CF_pct: 0.42\n      E_F1: 83.89\n      E_P: 84.90\n      E_R: 82.91\n      R_F1: 43.68\n      R_P: 38.55\n      R_R: 50.36\n      parse_fail_rate: 2.30\n      total_samples: 998\n      total_entities_pred: 19098\n      total_relations_pred: 16158\n    result_rationale: >\n      Llama FT unconstrained already achieves DR=0.49% (even lower than Qwen's 0.76%), with only ~79\n      residual DR violations across 16171 relations. The lower baseline reflects Llama's stronger\n      alignment with DocRED's type system post-FT (also evidenced by its lower RV=0.15% vs Qwen 0.27%).\n      \n      DR→0.03% (not exactly 0%, ~5 residual violations) because Llama's FT residuals may include \n      a handful of edge cases where the semantic layer's fallback mechanism triggers. However, this is\n      within the [0.0, 0.1] best-tier band. The near-zero residual matches SciERC FT pattern exactly\n      (Llama SciERC FT+constrained achieved DR=0.09%).\n      \n      R-F1 Δ=-0.04pp (43.72→43.68) — negligible and within ±0.2pp band. The tiny negative delta occurs\n      because Llama's RV baseline is already very low (0.15%), so grammar provides minimal FP-elimination\n      benefit, while the semantic layer's rare interventions on ~79 triples occasionally redirect to a\n      type that doesn't match gold. This is the same pattern as Llama SciERC FT+constrained (R-F1 45.45→45.47,\n      Δ=+0.02pp) — essentially noise around zero.\n      \n      CF=0.42% is slightly higher than Qwen (0.38%) but still well below the 0.5% threshold. Llama\n      showed higher CF sensitivity in zero-shot (12.02% on DocRED vs Qwen's 2.57%), but FT's calibration\n      effect suppresses this just as effectively. The small CF residual reflects Llama's slightly higher\n      tendency toward entity-type drift in complex documents.\n      \n      Parse failure rate holds at 2.3% (unchanged from unconstrained) because Llama's FT model already\n      produces well-structured JSON, and grammar constraints neither help nor hurt. This contrasts with\n      zero-shot Llama where constrained decoding increased parse failures (6.8%→9.1%) — FT resolves the\n      underlying generation instability that caused those failures.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Llama DocRED FT Composability Matrix\"\n        rows:\n          - condition: \"FT unconstrained\"\n            DR: 0.49\n            RV: 0.15\n            ET: 0.0\n            CF: 0.0\n            R_F1: 43.72\n            E_F1: 83.93\n          - condition: \"FT + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\"\n            DR: 0.03\n            RV: 0.0\n            ET: 0.0\n            CF: 0.42\n            R_F1: 43.68\n            E_F1: 83.89\n          - condition: \"Zero-shot + OntoCoDec (full_s1)\"\n            DR: 2.55\n            RV: 0.0\n            ET: 0.0\n            CF: 12.02\n            R_F1: 6.27\n            E_F1: 55.01\n      - title: \"Cross-Model FT+Constrained Summary (DocRED)\"\n        rows:\n          - model: \"Qwen3-8B\"\n            DR_unconstrained: 0.76\n            DR_constrained: 0.0\n            DR_reduction: \"100%\"\n            R_F1_delta_pp: \"+0.06\"\n            CF_constrained: 0.38\n            parse_fail_delta: \"-0.10\"\n          - model: \"Llama-3.1-8B\"\n            DR_unconstrained: 0.49\n            DR_constrained: 0.03\n            DR_reduction: \"93.9%\"\n            R_F1_delta_pp: \"-0.04\"\n            CF_constrained: 0.42\n            parse_fail_delta: \"0.00\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"2×2 composability heatmap: rows = {zero-shot, FT}, columns = {unconstrained, +OntoCoDec}. Cells show DR%. Pattern: FT reduces DR by ~97%, OntoCoDec eliminates remainder. Both models show same pattern.\"\n      - \"Line plot: CF rate across settings. Zero-shot+OntoCoDec shows high CF (Qwen 2.57%, Llama 12.02%); FT+OntoCoDec collapses CF to <0.5% for both models. Demonstrates FT calibration resolves CF limitation.\"\n      - \"Paired bar chart: R-F1 before/after OntoCoDec for FT models. Both models show bars of indistinguishable height (Δ < 0.1pp), visually confirming zero-cost composability.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Llama's results complete the 2×2×2 matrix (2 models × 2 datasets × 2 constraint settings for FT).\n      The composability claim is now supported by 4 independent datapoints, all showing the same pattern:\n      DR→≈0%, R-F1 within ±0.2pp, CF<0.5%.\n      \n      The most striking finding is the CF collapse: Llama went from 12.02% CF (zero-shot+constrained) to\n      0.42% (FT+constrained) — a 96.5% reduction. This transforms the CF limitation story entirely.\n      CF was never an architectural defect of OntoCoDec; it was a symptom of applying type constraints\n      to a model that hadn't learned the type system. Once the model is calibrated via FT, OntoCoDec's\n      per-relation enforcement produces consistent types because the model's own type priors are already\n      consistent — the semantic layer merely enforces a boundary the model rarely approaches.\n      \n      Paper narrative upgrade: \"OntoCoDec is a modular compliance layer. Applied to a zero-shot model,\n      it eliminates 90-99% of DR violations with elevated CF as a known trade-off. Applied to a fine-tuned\n      model, it eliminates 94-100% of residual DR at zero cost — no F1 loss, no CF elevation, no parse\n      failure increase. The two interventions are orthogonal and compose freely.\"\n      \n      This directly addresses C8 (low absolute F1): FT achieves 40-44% R-F1 (competitive with supervised\n      methods given the zero-shot-to-few-shot gap), and OntoCoDec adds guaranteed compliance on top.\n      The practical deployment story is complete: train your model, add OntoCoDec, get guaranteed\n      type-valid output at zero marginal cost.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21689,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21689},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e30e0832-167e-4ad8-88b3-acd7e7e0f86f","uuid":"687a2dff-2bdc-4828-9cee-fc9d01ba4297"}
